WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.589 --> 00:00:05.670
American Sniper is currently one of the most
popular movies in the country. It's about

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:10.100
the life of Chris Kyle, the sniper with the
most confirmed kills in American military

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:15.630
history. It focuses on his life, his failures,
his successes, and the trauma that he experienced

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:19.660
as a soldier. But viewers of the movie may
be surprised at the way that it talks about

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:24.750
the Iraq War in general. From the very beginning
of Kyle's military career, it's about a response

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:28.880
to terrorism. He joins the military after
we see the bombings of American embassies

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:33.780
in Kenya and Tanzania. And we see his and
his wife Taya's stunned reaction to 9/11.

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:40.400
But then bam. Shortly after that...  "I just got the call, boys. It's on!"

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:48.219
Kyle's at war in Iraq. There's no intervening time spent on George W. Bush, weapons of mass destruction
(WMD), or Saddam Hussein. The implication

00:00:48.219 --> 00:00:53.879
that the viewer gets is that the invasion
of Iraq was a logical response to 9/11. 

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:03.940
Of course, that's not actually what happened.
We know that after 9/11, the US invaded Afghanistan,

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:09.219
were Al Qaeda actually was. The decision to
invade Iraq came as a result of a preexisting political

00:01:09.219 --> 00:01:13.979
project that members of the Bush administration
had and was sold on the pretense of weapons

00:01:13.979 --> 00:01:18.709
of mass destruction in Iraq. "The main reason
we went into Iraq at the time was we thought

00:01:18.709 --> 00:01:22.529
he had weapons of mass destruction. It turns
out he didn't, but he had the capacity to

00:01:22.529 --> 00:01:27.389
make weapons of -- but I also talked about
the human suffering in Iraq. And I also talked

00:01:27.389 --> 00:01:31.569
the need to advance a freedom agenda." In
the movie's narrative, there's no intervening

00:01:31.569 --> 00:01:36.259
time between the American invasion and Kyle
fighting Al Qaeda in Iraq. "He's trained by

00:01:36.259 --> 00:01:43.979
bin Laden. He's loyal to bin Laden. His name
is Zarqawi. Now this asshole is right now

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:49.420
the crown price of Al Qaeda in Iraq." That
leaves viewers with the impression that the

00:01:49.429 --> 00:01:52.639
Iraq War was against Al Qaeda at the outset
and that America had invaded Iraq because

00:01:52.639 --> 00:01:57.359
it had become a hotbed of Al Qaeda operations.
The reverse is true. Al Qaeda in Iraq grew

00:01:57.359 --> 00:02:02.859
out of the American invasion. It was weak
and wasn't even Al Qaeda in Iraq when the

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:08.770
invasion happened. Its leader, Abu Musab
al-Zarqawi, correctly assessed that the US

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:13.530
invasion would allow him to build up his group.
Eastwood wanted to make a movie about one

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:17.680
guy. He didn't set out to make a movie about
the Iraq War. The problem is, though he himself

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:22.060
is anti-war, he ended up letting the film
get wrapped up in Kyle's perspective. "You

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:26.810
want these motherfuckers to come to San Diego
or New York? We're protecting more than just

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:32.599
this dirt." While it's easy to go sit at a
movie, especially one that tries hard not

00:02:32.599 --> 00:02:38.010
to shove politics in your face, and appreciate
it as simply a movie about heroism, this movie

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:43.350
isn't just that. It's a movie that's going
to leave viewers with a false perception of what happened.

